title: "groundwork: a research pipeline whose first stage refuses the project"
abstract: "Autonomous research pipelines are cheap at everything downstream of a decision to proceed - survey, ideation, implementation, writing - and the expensive failures are all upstream of it. This is a seven-stage pipeline for coding agents whose first stage is a gate: four measurements taken before the first real experiment (a strongest trivial baseline, an oracle, a random arm, a positive control) that refuse a direction when the headroom is under two standard errors, when a perfect method would still be reported as null on the available split, when a random policy reaches the baseline, or when a positive control fails to recover. It ships an archive of ten ways a direction dies, each with the cheap test that would have ended it sooner; a pre-registration tool that checks with version control that the registration was committed before the results it governs; cluster tooling that places work on genuinely idle hardware and refuses to split one experimental arm across two GPU models; and three verification layers that are blind to different classes of defect."
type: software
